1109 0 obj
<<
/Title (Types: Functional, Product, Organizational, External Requirements, Eliciting Requirements, Developing Use-cases, Building requirement models, Negotiation, Validation.)
/Dest [28 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1106 0 R
/Prev 1108 0 R
>>
endobj
1110 0 obj
<<
/Title (1\) Functional: In software engineering, "functional" refers to the specific features and behaviors a system must exhibit to perform its intended tasks and meet user needs, detailing what the software should do. These are also known as functional requirements and describe actions like data processing, user interactions, calculations, and other specific operations, forming the core functionality that developers build the system around.)
/Dest [29 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1106 0 R
/Next 1112 0 R
>>
endobj
1111 0 obj
<<
/Title (6. Developing Use-cases: Developing use cases in software engineering involves creating detailed descriptions of how a system interacts with its users or other external entities to achieve specific goals. This process is crucial for capturing functional requirements and ensuring the developed software meets user needs.)
/Dest [30 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1113 0 R
/Next 1107 0 R
>>
endobj
1112 0 obj
<<
/Title (2\) Product: Product Requirements Document \(PRD\) is a fundamental aspect of product management, which plays a critical role in the development of successful products. Product requirements in software engineering are high-level descriptions of a product's desired features, functions, and behaviors, defining what the product should do to meet user and business goals, rather than how it should be implemented.)
/Dest [29 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1110 0 R
/Next 1114 0 R
>>
endobj
1113 0 obj
<<
/Title (5. Eliciting Requirements: The process of investigating and learning about a system's requirements from users, clients, and other stakeholders is known as requirements elicitation. Requirements elicitation in software engineering is perhaps the most difficult, most error-prone, and most communication-intensive software development.)
/Dest [30 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1115 0 R
/Next 1111 0 R
>>
endobj
1114 0 obj
<<
/Title (3\) Organizational: Organizational requirements in software engineering are the needs, policies, and objectives of a business that the software must satisfy to support its operations effectively, encompassing aspects like business goals, compliance, integration, security, scalability, and user experience.)
/Dest [29 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1112 0 R
/Next 1115 0 R
>>
endobj
1115 0 obj
<<
/Title (4. External Requirements: In software engineering, external requirements define aspects that originate outside the system itself but are necessary for its proper interaction with its environment and stakeholders. They typically encompass external interfaces, such as user, software, and communication interfaces, which dictate how the system interacts with users, other applications, and external hardware or networks.)
/Dest [30 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1114 0 R
/Next 1113 0 R
>>
endobj
